cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "CoRTeC: A Differentially Private Release for Synthetic Tabular Data, Decodable by Any Generator"
message: "If you use this software or the accompanying paper, please cite it."
type: software
license: Apache-2.0
version: "1.2.5"
date-released: "2026-10-30"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22945315"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Calyie/cortec"
abstract: >-
  We propose CoRTeC, a differentially private release for synthetic tabular data that separates
  spending the privacy budget from generating rows. CoRTeC spends the budget once on a small
  release of noisy counts designed for training a classifier. For each cohort of the data the
  release gives the cohort's size, its class balance, a histogram of every attribute for each
  outcome class and the outcome rate in a set of non-overlapping cells. A reviewer can check every
  one of these numbers before the release leaves the institution. Everything after the release is
  post-processing and costs no further privacy. A maximum-entropy decoder can turn the counts into
  rows. So can each of the five frozen language models we tested, and none of them ever sees a
  record. A selection step then matches the decoded rows to the released counts. One release can
  therefore serve any number of draws by any team with any model, and the model can be replaced
  without touching the guarantee. Decoded without a language model, the auto-configured release at
  ε = 2 trains classifiers to within 0.02 AUC of a real sample of the same size on Adult over
  three splits. The gap is larger on NHANES and bank marketing, reaching 0.07 AUC on the medical
  dataset. The fixed workload carries the conditional rates a deployment names at some cost to
  pairs of columns. Four controls show where the utility comes from: the release and the selection
  step provide most of it, and a language model adds what it knows from the column names. No
  private row ever reaches a decoder and no synthetic row copies a training row. The strongest
  membership attack stays far below the bound the budget allows. This repository holds the
  Apache-2.0 reference implementation with its tests, the research pipeline, the paper, the
  deployment guide and the frozen auto-configurations.
authors:
